 ## Overview
-Interpolation Methods approximate a continuous scalar field $V(\mathbf{p})$ from discrete voxel samples $V_i$ to determine values at arbitrary query positions $\mathbf{p}$ along viewing rays [S1, S2, unofficial]. The choice of scheme dictates the trade-off between computational cost and visual fidelity, ranging from the blocky, artifact-prone nearest-neighbor method to high-quality, computationally expensive higher-order filters like tri-cubic B-splines or windowed sinc functions . Unlike [Interpolation Methods](10526-interpolation-methods.md) which covers general mathematical techniques, this family focuses on reconstruction filters specifically optimized for volumetric data, including frequency-domain resampling and hardware-accelerated approximations.
